You are supporting a PhD thesis literature review (Chapter 2) on Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). Thesis context you must keep in mind: the thesis studies (1) how retrieval mechanisms can provide the most relevant context for knowledge-intensive generation tasks, (2) how retrieval granularity (sentence / paragraph / document / summary level) affects generation quality, and (3) how RAG systems should be evaluated beyond end-task accuracy (attribution quality, retrieval relevance, faithfulness). The chapter requires a methodology comparison across five key papers.

YOUR TASK: deep-analyze exactly ONE paper. Paper assignment: Original RAG formulation paper: 'Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks' by Lewis et al., arXiv ID 2005.11401 (2020).

Steps to perform (use only read/search/download scholarly tools):
1. LOCATE: use mcp__arxiv_local__search_papers with the given arXiv ID / title keywords to find the paper in the database. Judge every search result by its title AND abstract: accept it only if it is genuinely about retrieval-augmented generation; if any result you encounter is clearly NOT about RAG (e.g., a paper from an unrelated field), explicitly note in your report that you rejected it and why. Do not analyze non-RAG papers.
2. DOWNLOAD: use mcp__arxiv_local__download_paper with paper_id = the arXiv ID (retry with check_status=true if the first call reports conversion in progress), then read the full converted content with mcp__arxiv_local__read_paper.
3. SOURCE MANUSCRIPT: examine the LaTeX source for methodology detail — use mcp__arxiv-latex__list_paper_sections (same arXiv ID), then mcp__arxiv-latex__get_paper_section on the methodology / model / approach / experimental-setup sections. Also fetch mcp__arxiv-latex__get_paper_abstract to confirm the abstract. If the LaTeX source is unavailable for this ID, say so clearly and base the methodology discussion on the downloaded paper content instead.
4. REPORT (return everything in your final report text; you are a read-only agent — do NOT write, create, modify, or append to ANY file, and in particular do NOT create or touch any .docx file): provide:
   (a) exact paper title; (b) full author list; (c) publication year; (d) arXiv ID as listed in the database;
   (e) MAIN CONTRIBUTION: 4-6 sentences describing what the paper introduces and why it matters;
   (f) METHODOLOGY: a detailed paragraph (or two) covering the approach/architecture, training or construction procedure, datasets, evaluation setup and metrics — written so it can anchor a methodology-comparison section;
   (g) KEY RESULTS: 2-4 sentences with concrete numbers where available;
   (h) THESIS FIT: 1-2 sentences relating the paper to the three thesis themes above;
   (i) a note on which search results (if any) you judged non-RAG and rejected.
